<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yamaguchi Prefecture</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yamaguchi Prefecture is the westernmost prefecture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, with its capital at Yamaguchi city and its largest city at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimonoseki">Shimonoseki</a>. It was formed in 1871 from the Chōshū domain of the Mōri clan, covering the old provinces of Suō and Nagato. Its men led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>, and it has produced more prime ministers of Japan than any other prefecture, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ito-hirobumi">Itō Hirobumi</a>, Satō Eisaku and Abe Shinzō.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yamaguchi-prefecture.jpg" alt="Yamaguchi Prefecture" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: កៀនស្វាយ · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B2%A9%E5%9B%BD%E5%9F%8E%E3%81%8B%E3%82%89%E8%A6%8B%E3%81%9F%E9%8C%A6%E5%B8%AF%E6%A9%8B.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Yamaguchi Prefecture lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chugoku-region">Chūgoku region</a> at the western tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>. It has coasts on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a> and the Seto Inland Sea, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanmon-straits">Kanmon Straits</a> separate it from the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a>. It covers 6,112 km2 and borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimane-prefecture">Shimane Prefecture</a> to the north and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima-prefecture">Hiroshima Prefecture</a> to the northeast. Besides Yamaguchi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimonoseki">Shimonoseki</a>, the major cities include Ube, Shūnan and Iwakuni. Its position on the straits has made it a link to northern Kyushu, and it has long been tied to the Kanmon Straits metropolitan area.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The prefecture was created by the merger of the provinces of Suō and Nagato. During the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> in the Heian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> periods, the Ouchi family of Suō and the Koto family of Nagato became powerful warrior clans. In the Muromachi period Ouchi Hiroyo, the 24th ruler of the Ouchi, conquered both provinces. The Ouchi modelled their city on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> and grew wealthy from cultural imports from the continent and trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and Ming China, so that Yamaguchi came to be called the Kyoto of the West.</p><p>Sue Harutaka defeated the 31st Ouchi ruler, and the Sue were in turn defeated by Mōri Motonari, whose family came to control the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chugoku-region">Chūgoku region</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mori-terumoto">Mōri Terumoto</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> in 1600, Terumoto was forced to give up all his land except Suō and Nagato, where he built his castle at Hagi. The Mōri promoted salt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and paper in a campaign known as the Three Whites.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthew-c-perry">Commodore Matthew Perry</a> opened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, clans from Nagato, also called Chōshū, played a key part in the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> and the setting up of the new imperial government. Shipbuilding, chemical, machinery and metalworking plants were built in the harbours of the Seto Inland Sea in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taisho-period">Taishō period</a> (1912–1926), and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> petrochemical complexes made it one of the most industrialised prefectures in Japan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The prefecture had a population of 1,377,631 in 2018, and its population has fallen in most years since 1985. Politicians from Yamaguchi, drawing on the Chōshū role in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>, have held many high offices; the Kishi, Satō and Abe family forms the best-known prime ministerial line of the post-war era. Visitors go to the five-arched wooden Kintai Bridge at Iwakuni, the Akiyoshido cave, said to be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s longest, and Hagi, whose reverberatory furnace is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yamaguchi Prefecture” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its men, Kido Takayoshi, Ito Hirobumi and Yamagata Aritomo, led Meiji Japan; it became <b>Yamaguchi Prefecture</b> in 1871.</blockquote><a href="/state/choshu-domain">Choshu Domain</a></li></ul></section>
